    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">3000 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">First settlement is established near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gihon-spring">Gihon Spring</a>, according to archaeological evidence</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1330s BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdi-heba">Abdi-Heba</a> name a city called Urusalim</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">586 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> captures Jerusalem and burns the Temple</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">164 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-maccabeus">Judas Maccabeus</a> captures Jerusalem and restores temple worship</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">70</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> destroys much of Jerusalem and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> after a five-month siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">638</span><span class="rf-text">Jerusalem is conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1099</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> take Jerusalem and make it capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1967</span><span class="rf-text">Israel takes East Jerusalem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amarna-letters.jpg" alt="Amarna letters" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Amarna_letters_on_display_at_the_British_Museum,_LondonA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jerusalem stands on the southern spur of a plateau in the Judaean Mountains, about 760 m above sea level in the Old City, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a>. The Kidron, Hinnom and Tyropoeon valleys meet just south of the Old City, and the Tyropoeon once divided the lower city into two hills, the Temple Mount to the east and the rest of the city to the west. Water was always scarce, and the first settlement was tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gihon-spring">Gihon Spring</a>: archaeological evidence suggests it was established there between 3000 and 2800 BCE.</p><p>The name is variously explained as "foundation of the god Shalem", Shalem being a Canaanite god of dusk. Egyptian Execration Texts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom</a> may refer to the city, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdi-heba">Abdi-Heba</a>, from the 1330s BCE, name an Urusalim, which may be the earliest certain mention. By the 17th century BCE the Canaanites had built massive walls on the eastern side to protect the water system. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> the city was one of several small city states that were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, it was then called Jebus and its independent Canaanite inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebusites">Jebusites</a>; the Books of Samuel say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> took it from them and made it the City of David.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Temple in Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_Modell_BW_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Berthold Werner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> places the city's rise in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, and says that Solomon built the First Temple. Archaeologists are divided on whether the excavations support this: Eilat Mazar contends that her digging uncovered large stone buildings of the right period, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein">Israel Finkelstein</a> disputes both her interpretation and the dating. Radiocarbon dates show that Jerusalem was relatively densely inhabited in the 12th to 10th centuries BCE, with a major expansion to the west beginning in the 9th century BCE. Thomas L. Thompson argues that it could act as a state capital only from the middle of the 7th century BCE, whereas Omer Sergi holds that it was already a significant city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> IIA.</p><p>Under the Hasmoneans, who ruled an independent state from Jerusalem, the number of rural production sites within a two hour walk of the Temple Mount nearly doubled, from 15 to 29. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, who took the city in 37 BCE, rebuilt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> and more than doubled the size of the surrounding complex, so that the Temple Mount became the largest temenos in the ancient world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called Jerusalem "the most famous by far of the Eastern cities". By the end of the Second Temple period the city had, by modern estimates, 70,000 to 100,000 people, a peak that was not broken until the 20th century.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Jerusalem&#8221; cites where it covers Demographic history, Economy:</p><ul><li>Michael Dumper, The Politics of Jerusalem Since 1967 (Columbia University Press, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, Jerusalem was the capital of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, and after the kingdom divided it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, which it remained for some 400 years until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> captured it in 586 BCE. After the Persian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were allowed to return, and the city lay within the Persian province of Yehud. Alexander's conquests brought Greek rule in 332 BCE, then Ptolemaic control, and from 198 BCE Seleucid. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabean-revolt">Maccabean revolt</a> gave Jerusalem back as the capital of the Hasmonean state until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> took it in 63 BCE. Herod ruled it as a client king from 37 BCE, and direct Roman rule followed in 6 CE.</p><p>After 70 CE it became the Roman colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a>, then a Byzantine city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> held it from 614 to 629, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> reconquered it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> took it in 638, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. Seljuk forces captured it in 1071, the Fatimids retook it in 1098, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took it in July 1099 and made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> recaptured it in 1187, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> held it from 1260 until 1516. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ruled it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-allenby">Edmund Allenby</a> entered on 11 December 1917, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> administered it under a Mandate from 1922 to 1948.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Jerusalem&#8221; cites where it covers Status under Israeli rule:</p><ul><li>Moshe Amirav, Jerusalem Syndrome: The Palestinian-Israeli Battle for the Holy City (Sussex Academic Press, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre.jpg" alt="Church of the Holy Sepulchre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_by_Gerd_Eichmann_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerd Eichmann, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jerusalem's life has long been shaped by its holy places and the pilgrims who came to them. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a> Hadrian placed the forum at the junction of the Cardo and the Decumanus and built a temple to Jupiter Capitolinus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were banned from the city. In Byzantine times the burial remains are exclusively Christian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a>, who visited in 1173, described a small city full of Jacobites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, with two hundred Jews living in a corner under the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> left about 90 surviving structures from the 13th to 15th centuries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, libraries, hospitals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, fountains and public baths, mostly around the edge of the Temple Mount. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> the walls that still define the Old City were built, and Jews, Christians and Muslims could find a synagogue, a church and a mosque on one street. In the mid-19th century the population was divided into four main communities, Jewish, Christian, Muslim and Armenian, each living mainly near its shrines. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> was partitioned among the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Catholic, Armenian, Coptic and Ethiopian churches, and its keys were kept by two Muslim families regarded as neutral. During the Mandate a rule requiring buildings to be faced with sandstone preserved the look of the city as it grew.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Jerusalem&#8221; cites where it covers Religious significance, Judaism, Christianity, Islam:</p><ul><li>David E. Guinn, Protecting Jerusalem's Holy Sites: A Strategy for Negotiating a Sacred Peace (Cambridge University Press, 2006)</li><li>Breger Marshall J., Jerusalem: A City and Its Future (Syracuse University Press, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sennacherib.jpg" alt="Sennacherib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanherib-tr-4271.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Timo Roller, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By one count, Jerusalem has been destroyed completely at least twice, besieged 23 times, captured and recaptured 44 times and attacked 52 times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> besieged it in 701 BCE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> says an angel killed 185,000 men in his army, while Sennacherib's own account says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> was shut up like a caged bird and paid tribute. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> took it in 597 BCE and again in 586 BCE, when they burnt the Temple and destroyed the walls. In 70 CE, after a five-month siege, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a>'s legions destroyed much of the city and burnt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt, Hadrian put down the rebellion, in which as many as half a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> are said to have been killed; Jews were banned from the city on pain of death, except on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisha-bav">Tisha B'Av</a>.</p><p>In 614 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> took the city after 21 days; Byzantine chronicles say they and the Jews killed tens of thousands of Christians and destroyed churches, an episode historians have debated. In the early 11th century the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim ordered all churches destroyed. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took the city in July 1099 they massacred almost all of its Muslim and Jewish inhabitants. In 1219 the Ayyubid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> ordered the walls razed. In 1948 the Jewish population was besieged, civilians were killed on both sides, and Arab villages and districts of the west were taken and their residents displaced. After taking East Jerusalem in June 1967, Israel demolished the Moroccan Quarter, destroying 135 homes and 14 religious buildings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock.jpg" alt="Dome of the Rock" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jerusalem_DomeoftheRock_J65_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ludvig14, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jerusalem is holy to three faiths. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> the Temple Mount, site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>'s Temple and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>, is held to be the holiest site, and prayers are said facing it. Christians revere the city for its place in the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> holds the sites where, in Christian belief, he was crucified, buried and raised from the dead. Muslims hold that Muhammad travelled by night from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to the farthest place of prayer, which Islamic scholars later understood as Jerusalem, and that Jerusalem was the first direction of prayer; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> and the Qibli (al-Aqsa) Mosque stand on the compound.</p><p>The Old City, of less than 1 square kilometre, became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1981. The city's status is disputed. Israel holds that Jerusalem is its complete and united capital, as its 1980 Jerusalem Law declares, while Palestinians regard East Jerusalem as the capital of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>; most of the international community regards East Jerusalem as occupied Palestinian territory, and the Security Council called the 1980 law null and void. The city has over a million people, about three fifths of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and almost two fifths Palestinians.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Jerusalem&#8221; cites where it covers Religious significance, Judaism, Christianity, Islam:</p><ul><li>David E. Guinn, Protecting Jerusalem's Holy Sites: A Strategy for Negotiating a Sacred Peace (Cambridge University Press, 2006)</li><li>Breger Marshall J., Jerusalem: A City and Its Future (Syracuse University Press, 2002)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137<span>c. 570 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs<span>c. 250 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)<span>c. 300</span></a><a href="/songs/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi<span>c. 1550</span></a><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)<span>1842</span></a><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah<span>1878</span></a><a href="/songs/hava-nagila">Hava Nagila<span>1918</span></a><a href="/songs/jerusalem-of-gold">Jerusalem of Gold<span>1967</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Jerusalem is a city of the Judaean Mountains that has been settled since about 3000 BCE and is holy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. It has been besieged, destroyed and rebuilt many times under many rulers, from Canaanite and Judahite kings to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, Muslim dynasties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the British, and its political status is disputed today.</em></p>

